WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.640
A safe RAM-boot design routine starts with the goal. Do you want a live system that can

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.300
run after boot media is removed? A resettable lab machine?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.280
A kiosk that returns to a clean state? A low-wear system that reduces writes? A recovery

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:16.460
environment?

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.120
Name the goal first,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.500
because the goal determines the pattern and the persistence design. Next,

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:23.840
choose the pattern.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.900
Live-to-RAM designs copy a base image into memory. Overlay designs combine a read-only

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.600
lower layer with a writable upper layer.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.420
Selected volatile paths move only certain directories into tmpfs. Hybrid designs keep

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.000
chosen data persistent while allowing other changes to reset.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:47.820
Avoid copying commands without understanding which pattern they implement. Understand the

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.000
boot path.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.780
Firmware starts the machine, the bootloader loads the kernel and initramfs,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.880
early user space prepares devices and filesystems,

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.820
and the system eventually switches to the root filesystem.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.920
RAM-backed designs often affect that early path,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.300
so distribution details matter. Estimate RAM and volatility.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.920
The base image, overlay, cache, desktop, applications, containers, browser activity,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.140
and updates all compete for memory.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.480
Decide what disappears at reboot and what survives.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:25.380
Plan /home, logs, package state, configuration, updates, SSH keys,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.200
and application data deliberately. Build in a lab first.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:35.740
Test boot, workload, memory pressure, shutdown, reboot, update behavior, persistence,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.600
and recovery. Keep recovery media. Keep a known-good boot entry.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:46.020
Back up important configuration. Document exactly what changed and how to roll it back.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.680
Only then consider using the design on a daily or production-like system. RAM booting is

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.260
not magic and not one feature. It is a design choice.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:59.640
When the design matches the workload and includes a recovery plan, it can be useful,

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.940
clean, and reversible.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.520
When it is improvised on a primary machine, it can be fragile.
